From eeca3eb493b8a37b1c3e0b8e7e3a3a7e3a3a7e3a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dave Seddon <dave.seddon.ca@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 1 Jun 2026 16:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH RFC 0/3] net: flow_dissector: fast-path for common L3+L4 shapes
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A small targeted optimisation to __skb_flow_dissect(): add a
straight-line extractor at the top of the function for the most
common packet shapes, before entering the slow-path graph walk.

  - What this does. Adds a fast-path for eth+IPv4+{TCP,UDP} and
    eth+IPv6+{TCP,UDP} packets. On a match it fills struct
    flow_keys byte-identically to the slow path and returns.
    On any miss it defers to the existing graph walk. Only the
    two standard dissectors are eligible (flow_keys_dissector and
    flow_keys_dissector_symmetric); custom dissectors defer.
    No new API, no ABI change, no header changes.

  - How it works. A single ethertype branch routes to a per-family
    helper. The helper does a fixed set of checks (version + IHL or
    extension headers, fragmentation, nexthdr), then writes
    key_control, key_basic, key_addrs, and key_ports from fixed
    offsets. Total +207 lines, all in net/core/flow_dissector.c.
    Fall-through cost on a miss is one ethertype switch (~5 cycles).
    BPF flow_dissector override semantics are preserved -- the
    fast-path runs only after the existing BPF check decides no BPF
    program is loaded for this netns.

  - Why it is faster. The slow-path graph walk dispatches through
    per-protocol function pointers and a linear lookup_node table
    per layer. For the common shapes the fast-path bypasses all of
    that. Userspace microbench (libflowdis port, synthetic
    eth+IPv4+TCP, 10 M iterations, gcc -O3):

      uarch    host                          baseline   patched   delta
      -----    ----                          --------   -------   -----
      Zen 2    Threadripper PRO 3945WX N=10  12.44 ns   6.56 ns   -47.3 %
      Zen 1    Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G N=5         20.50 ns   20.53 ns  noise

    On Zen 1 the saving is below the clock_gettime resolution at
    p50 (visible at p10); cycles are saved either way.

Output byte-equivalence is enforced by field-by-field matching of
the slow-path writes. A kernel selftest for byte-by-byte comparison
is planned for v2.

Tested
======

  - scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict: 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks
    per patch.
  - W=1 build clean per patch.
  - sparse (master) and smatch (1.74) clean on
    net/core/flow_dissector.c.
  - Four-host testbed, Ryzen 5 PRO 2400G (Zen 1, 4c/8t):
    * Pair 1: hp2 (gen) <-> hp5 (DUT), Intel X710 10 GbE i40e
    * Pair 2: hp1 (gen) <-> hp3 (DUT), Mellanox CX-4 Lx 25 GbE
  - 16 macro cells (iperf3 + iperf2 x IPv4/IPv6 x TCP/UDP x 2
    pairs, 60 s each, sch_cake triple-isolate, tuned TCP). All
    cells pass; no regression vs the series 1+2 B.1 24h baseline
    on mlx5.
  - 24h soaks, both pairs in parallel:
      i40e iperf3: 279 runs, 9.4114 Gbit/s mean, CV 0.011 %,
                   drift -0.000 %, 0 cake drops.
      mlx5 iperf2: 284 runs, 16.258 Gbit/s mean, CV 1.68 %,
                   drift -0.504 %, 7 cake drops, +11 MB RSS.
    0 new dmesg WARN/BUG/oops on either host throughout.

This is sent as RFC for feedback on:

  - The dispatch shape (single ethertype switch with per-family
    helpers).
  - The byte-exact output rule (vs the cheaper alternative of
    skipping "cold" slot writes that no current consumer reads).
  - The dissector-identity check (only the two standard
    dissectors, vs a broader rule based on key-set subset).

